00:01:55.885 --> 00:01:59.355
The first experiment that you
performed was making measurements

00:01:59.355 --> 00:02:03.705
back in week, week one or week two of
your semester, a very long time ago.

00:02:04.195 --> 00:02:07.455
So with this lab, it went over
some core concepts that were used

00:02:07.455 --> 00:02:09.059
throughout the entire semester.

00:02:09.340 --> 00:02:11.640
The biggest thing was is you need
to learn how to make different

00:02:11.650 --> 00:02:14.290
measurements of mass, volume, and length.

00:02:14.740 --> 00:02:18.230
So when we're making those measurements,
the biggest thing is when you're taking

00:02:18.230 --> 00:02:23.219
your practical, you need to record all of
your significant figures and your units

00:02:23.240 --> 00:02:25.299
when you are writing down your answers.

00:02:25.625 --> 00:02:28.805
If you do not write one of those
things, you might lose points on

00:02:28.855 --> 00:02:31.835
questions, and you might have got
the answer right, but you didn't

00:02:31.835 --> 00:02:33.345
write your units or show your work.

00:02:33.644 --> 00:02:35.374
So make sure you write
all those things down.

00:02:35.374 --> 00:02:36.105
They're very important.

00:02:36.654 --> 00:02:40.475
So when you're writing anything down
from the balance, include all the digits.

00:02:40.484 --> 00:02:44.294
If you're measuring a volume, you've
got to read your tick marks correctly.

00:02:44.775 --> 00:02:48.165
So for example, I included a
ruler here just to kind of go

00:02:48.165 --> 00:02:49.575
through, like, how would you do it.

00:02:49.905 --> 00:02:53.285
So I put like a little block here,
which you had to measure that first lab.

00:02:53.665 --> 00:02:56.935
So if I were to do this, how
exactly would you go about it?

00:02:57.310 --> 00:03:00.260
So right now I have the block
going between one and two.

00:03:00.580 --> 00:03:04.100
So if we were to kind of look at this
thing, you can see, okay, there's 0.

00:03:04.150 --> 00:03:05.654
1, 0.

00:03:05.654 --> 00:03:06.301
2, 0.

00:03:06.301 --> 00:03:09.330
3, 4, 5, 6, 7, right?

00:03:09.330 --> 00:03:12.599
So the block ends at about 0 or 0.

00:03:12.599 --> 00:03:16.730
7 is where the difference
is in that measurement.

00:03:17.260 --> 00:03:19.570
So is that all you would put though?

00:03:19.970 --> 00:03:23.140
So the one thing you got to
remember is that there was certain.

00:03:23.770 --> 00:03:24.300
Digits.

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:27.960
So what you can see via the tick
marks, and then you could always

00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:31.090
estimate one beyond that tick mark.

00:03:31.450 --> 00:03:35.570
So that last digit is fully up
to you what you want to place,

00:03:35.950 --> 00:03:37.760
but you need to include it.

00:03:37.959 --> 00:03:39.730
So even though at 0.

00:03:39.730 --> 00:03:41.369
7, you, in this case, you'd have to put 0.

00:03:41.369 --> 00:03:45.300
70 to indicate the SIG figs, right?

00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:47.810
Trailing zeros are significant.

00:03:47.830 --> 00:03:49.140
So you need to place them.

00:03:49.530 --> 00:03:50.640
It matters, right?

00:03:50.650 --> 00:03:55.630
If you are using a very accurate piece of
equipment, you need to illustrate that and

00:03:55.630 --> 00:03:57.990
showcase that in your, in your vocabulary.

00:03:58.110 --> 00:04:01.400
I'm going to be kind of listing here
is like all the important videos and

00:04:01.400 --> 00:04:06.360
concepts and things that we, that me,
I made me made other videos on that you

00:04:06.360 --> 00:04:09.680
can refer to if you'd like, I'm going
to put them in the description too.

00:04:09.964 --> 00:04:13.484
So that way you can refer to them as
well in the YouTube video description.

00:04:14.005 --> 00:04:16.844
So the biggest thing is the
displacement method was one

00:04:16.844 --> 00:04:17.875
of the things that you used.

00:04:17.885 --> 00:04:22.624
So you all, you measured the volume via
length, but you also did the density

00:04:22.724 --> 00:04:24.875
by doing the displacement method too.

00:04:25.395 --> 00:04:29.245
So the one thing is when we went
over density, is what we refer

00:04:29.245 --> 00:04:31.625
to as an intrinsic property.

00:04:31.635 --> 00:04:33.575
And you're like, Oh crap,
I forgot about this stuff.

00:04:33.965 --> 00:04:37.425
So this meant that no matter what
the mass was or the volume, it

00:04:37.425 --> 00:04:39.145
would always have the same density.

00:04:39.154 --> 00:04:40.305
It does not change.

00:04:40.645 --> 00:04:44.425
So when, uh, essentially like a
couple months ago, I made different

00:04:44.425 --> 00:04:45.724
videos on how to go over this.

00:04:46.044 --> 00:04:49.175
One was what is density, which
just goes over the basic ideas.

00:04:49.465 --> 00:04:52.935
The other one was a displacement method
video, which actually goes through

00:04:52.935 --> 00:04:57.715
examples of this lab of how to show
how to, how to calculate these things.

00:04:58.390 --> 00:05:02.080
So when we're doing a displacement
method, it's actually quite simple.

00:05:02.489 --> 00:05:05.689
So the whole idea is just to kind
of scroll down here for a second.

00:05:06.190 --> 00:05:09.900
Was, essentially you were taking a
graduated cylinder with some water

00:05:09.900 --> 00:05:14.200
in it and you were dropping an
object into the grad cylinder and

00:05:14.200 --> 00:05:16.780
it displaced the volume of liquid.

00:05:17.200 --> 00:05:22.680
So one thing that it allows you to do
is, if you can remember, density is grams

00:05:22.719 --> 00:05:25.110
over milliliters or mass over volume.

00:05:25.480 --> 00:05:29.860
So what you can do is if you measure
the mass and then you measure the

00:05:29.860 --> 00:05:35.250
initial volume, then you drop the
object in the liquid, it will displace.

00:05:35.825 --> 00:05:36.705
that liquid, right?

00:05:37.305 --> 00:05:41.285
So now that displacement is
the volume of your object.

00:05:41.685 --> 00:05:46.325
So one thing that you had to do is you
had to take that final volume, minus

00:05:46.325 --> 00:05:50.174
it by your initial, and then plug
it into your density formula, right?

00:05:50.174 --> 00:05:52.674
So grams over that displacement.

00:05:53.160 --> 00:05:54.600
And that would allow you to do density.

00:05:55.070 --> 00:05:58.460
So one thing that when we do this,
right, you have to make sure that

00:05:58.470 --> 00:06:02.700
the volume is high enough to submerge
your object, but not too high where

00:06:02.700 --> 00:06:04.750
like it goes over your tick marks.

00:06:04.989 --> 00:06:06.339
So keep that in mind, right?

00:06:06.340 --> 00:06:09.429
This is something that is
doable in four minutes, right?

00:06:09.430 --> 00:06:12.430
For you to quickly fill the grad
cylinder, maybe put something in it.

00:06:12.550 --> 00:06:13.310
This is doable.

00:06:13.320 --> 00:06:15.760
Same thing with measuring
an object, right?

00:06:15.760 --> 00:06:16.760
And calculating the volume.

00:06:17.095 --> 00:06:19.775
Now, keep in mind the formulas
would be given to you if you had

00:06:19.775 --> 00:06:24.485
a cylinder or a rectangle, but you
still need to know what density is.

00:06:24.485 --> 00:06:27.114
That's something that probably wouldn't
be given to you because you should

00:06:27.115 --> 00:06:30.195
know it because you've been using it
pretty much for most of the semester.

00:06:30.685 --> 00:06:34.024
So let's say if I were to do a simple,
simple problem to figure this out.

00:06:34.234 --> 00:06:37.604
So let's say I did give you a piece
of solid material and you weighed

00:06:37.604 --> 00:06:41.185
it and you found out it weighed 422.

00:06:41.245 --> 00:06:42.955
1234 grams.

00:06:43.315 --> 00:06:47.245
So, and then you decide to place
the object into 50 milliliters

00:06:47.295 --> 00:06:49.575
of water in a grad cylinder.

00:06:49.905 --> 00:06:52.614
The water rises to a volume of 78.

00:06:52.664 --> 00:06:53.864
2 milliliters.

00:06:54.205 --> 00:06:55.405
What is the density?

00:06:55.885 --> 00:06:58.474
So the first thing you're going
to do is remember, it's mass

00:06:58.554 --> 00:07:00.794
over volume is your density.

00:07:01.214 --> 00:07:05.665
So when we are trying to do that, the
first thing we're going to do is we're

00:07:05.665 --> 00:07:09.115
going to put that mass that was measured,
and we're going to put that on top.

00:07:09.705 --> 00:07:13.124
And then we're going to divide
it by the displaced volume.

00:07:13.585 --> 00:07:17.614
So being that it's always final
minus initial, so we had 78.

00:07:17.645 --> 00:07:21.255
2 milliliters as our final, right?

00:07:21.645 --> 00:07:24.205
And then our initial was 50.

00:07:24.294 --> 00:07:25.389
0 milliliters.

00:07:25.920 --> 00:07:30.390
So we'll take those, put them in our
denominator, and that's something

00:07:30.390 --> 00:07:31.710
that's going to be subtracted.

00:07:32.580 --> 00:07:38.029
So now once we do that, we
subtract those, we divide 422.

00:07:38.080 --> 00:07:39.989
1234 divided by 28.

00:07:40.020 --> 00:07:42.059
2, which is that difference.

00:07:42.505 --> 00:07:46.144
What you're going to get is 14.

00:07:46.145 --> 00:07:49.305
9689 grams per ml, right?

00:07:49.305 --> 00:07:50.315
So that's what you'd want to write.

00:07:50.315 --> 00:07:51.885
You want to write your
units and your number.

00:07:52.355 --> 00:07:54.535
Now the thing is, did
I get it right though?

00:07:54.875 --> 00:07:59.275
So yes, my calculator spitted that
value out and sure, that's what it said.

00:07:59.475 --> 00:08:00.234
It must be right.

00:08:00.825 --> 00:08:03.645
Thing is though, you have to be
aware of how to round it too.

00:08:03.945 --> 00:08:05.415
So we got to look at our sig figs.

00:08:05.425 --> 00:08:08.985
So you can see that the mass had
a ton of sig figs, or seven here.

00:08:09.305 --> 00:08:13.145
But the volumes were down to three, so
we always go off our lowest sig figs.

00:08:13.700 --> 00:08:17.440
So what we would have to do
is round it to 3 sig figs.

00:08:17.450 --> 00:08:18.840
So right under the 9 here.

00:08:19.290 --> 00:08:23.980
So what it's going to be
is 15 grams per milliliter.

00:08:24.020 --> 00:08:25.929
And I include my units.

00:08:26.230 --> 00:08:29.780
You box off your answer so that
your professor can find it easily.

00:08:30.025 --> 00:08:33.865
So that way it shows your work and the
other thing to keep in mind is you want to

00:08:33.865 --> 00:08:38.825
include all your work, even if you get the
wrong answer, include your work, it could

00:08:38.845 --> 00:08:42.685
award you a lot of partial credit points,
especially if you were on the right

00:08:42.724 --> 00:08:45.454
track when you were doing this problem.

00:08:45.824 --> 00:08:49.164
Now, the key thing to keep
and look out for is make sure

00:08:49.164 --> 00:08:50.574
you did displacement, right?

00:08:50.574 --> 00:08:53.144
It's called the displacement
method for a reason.

00:08:53.384 --> 00:08:55.074
Make sure you subtract your volumes.

00:08:55.374 --> 00:08:57.325
That's usually the thing
that people forget the most.

