00:14:51.599 --> 00:14:55.599
Now, when it comes to molecular modeling,
which was experiment 6, this was one

00:14:55.599 --> 00:14:58.549
of the earliest labs you had to do
where you first started to learn about

00:14:58.609 --> 00:15:00.560
how chemicals are bonded together.

00:15:01.099 --> 00:15:04.189
So, the only thing you could
essentially pull from this lab

00:15:04.540 --> 00:15:06.680
is how to draw a Lewis structure.

00:15:07.020 --> 00:15:08.770
So, this could come in
a couple different ways.

00:15:09.010 --> 00:15:11.580
One, you could be given the
chemical formula and you have

00:15:11.580 --> 00:15:12.830
to draw the Lewis structure.

00:15:13.185 --> 00:15:16.984
The other one is maybe you're given the
model, and you have to draw what the

00:15:16.984 --> 00:15:18.824
Lewis structure would be of the model.

00:15:19.295 --> 00:15:22.785
Um, depending on your teacher, you
might have also had to identify

00:15:22.795 --> 00:15:27.415
the VSEPR structure, but if that is
something you have to do, you would

00:15:27.425 --> 00:15:31.134
be given a reference sheet to help
you identify the actual geometry.

00:15:31.665 --> 00:15:35.425
Um, but let's say if, if you had to,
let's practice this real quick, cause

00:15:35.425 --> 00:15:38.985
it may be, it's probably been a while
since you had to do Lewis structures.

00:15:39.465 --> 00:15:43.005
So the first thing you had to do is find
the central atom, which I always like

00:15:43.005 --> 00:15:45.245
to say is what has the most friends.

00:15:45.624 --> 00:15:50.789
So looking at your bonding trends,
oxygen can make up to two bonds.

00:15:51.110 --> 00:15:52.570
Hydrogen can make one.

00:15:52.910 --> 00:15:56.210
So in that case, oxygen
can have more friends.

00:15:56.720 --> 00:15:59.319
So when you draw this, the
first thing you're going to do

00:15:59.319 --> 00:16:02.729
is you're going to place all of
its valence electrons around it.

00:16:03.215 --> 00:16:08.155
Oxygen has six valence electrons,
so we're going to add six dots

00:16:08.495 --> 00:16:10.285
all around that structure.

00:16:11.475 --> 00:16:14.095
Now the next thing we're going to do
is we're going to place the hydrogens.

00:16:14.445 --> 00:16:18.575
And you always want to put the bonds
in line with where those single dots

00:16:18.594 --> 00:16:20.265
are, because your goal is a pair.

00:16:20.675 --> 00:16:22.954
So we're going to put a
hydrogen on the bottom here.

00:16:23.325 --> 00:16:24.715
and then a hydrogen on the left.

00:16:25.065 --> 00:16:28.395
And if you drew this and you put
them in another spot that's fine

00:16:28.705 --> 00:16:32.185
as long as it's showing that the,
that each hydrogen has one valence

00:16:32.185 --> 00:16:34.195
electron and they're on their own.

00:16:34.855 --> 00:16:37.195
Now the next thing you're going
to do is you're going to connect

00:16:37.285 --> 00:16:38.544
all these different bonds.

00:16:38.544 --> 00:16:42.945
So we have one bond that's right here
and then we have another one on the side.

00:16:43.685 --> 00:16:46.585
Now after you do that you want
to count to make sure you have

00:16:46.625 --> 00:16:48.045
all your valence electrons.

00:16:48.444 --> 00:16:48.765
Right.

00:16:48.765 --> 00:16:52.094
So we have eight valence
electrons for this molecule

00:16:52.125 --> 00:16:53.864
before we connected everything.

00:16:54.354 --> 00:16:56.954
So when you're doing this, you
just want to kind of double check,

00:16:56.974 --> 00:16:58.894
make sure you got everything right.

00:16:58.894 --> 00:17:02.704
We have one, two, three, four,
five, six, then seven and eight.

00:17:02.964 --> 00:17:04.334
So everything's accounted for.

00:17:04.444 --> 00:17:05.644
We have a correct structure.

00:17:06.594 --> 00:17:11.355
Now with CO2, once again, a lot of times
that the central atom is written first.

00:17:11.740 --> 00:17:14.200
So in this case, carbon is your central.

00:17:14.720 --> 00:17:17.940
So carbon has four valence
electrons around it, so we're

00:17:17.940 --> 00:17:19.610
going to write those four dots.

00:17:20.059 --> 00:17:23.160
And then what we're going to do is
we're going to place our oxygens

00:17:23.170 --> 00:17:25.890
on each side, just to kind of
make it a little symmetrical.

00:17:26.740 --> 00:17:31.150
And then we're going to add the
six dots on each oxygen because

00:17:31.160 --> 00:17:34.030
it has six valence electrons.

00:17:34.040 --> 00:17:34.840
So we got that.

00:17:35.679 --> 00:17:38.940
Now the thing about these is
if you remember is that you all

00:17:38.940 --> 00:17:40.709
want to obey the octet rule.

00:17:41.029 --> 00:17:43.780
So we'll connect everything
with a single line first.

00:17:44.110 --> 00:17:48.060
And what you're going to see is
nothing obeys that rule of eight.

00:17:48.230 --> 00:17:52.000
The carbons don't, the oxygen, oxygen
stones, they're all really mad.

00:17:52.570 --> 00:17:55.280
So what we're going to do
is we're going to make.

00:17:55.715 --> 00:18:00.804
Double bonds first to see, hey, can
you make everything more satisfied?

00:18:01.205 --> 00:18:05.155
And when we make one double bond, it
makes the oxygen on the right happy.

00:18:05.565 --> 00:18:08.395
And then let's say if we make
a double bond on the left, it's

00:18:08.405 --> 00:18:09.544
also going to make that happy.

00:18:09.625 --> 00:18:12.205
They all have eight now
that they're all sharing.

00:18:12.575 --> 00:18:14.175
Carbon has four.

00:18:14.214 --> 00:18:17.675
It's giving four electrons via bonding
and it's receiving four as bonding.

00:18:17.675 --> 00:18:18.515
So it has all eight.

00:18:18.990 --> 00:18:22.410
And then you see the two lone pairs
around oxygen, and then you have the

00:18:22.430 --> 00:18:26.240
four that are being shared in the double
bond, so it has its complete structure.

00:18:26.630 --> 00:18:30.920
So this would be how you draw the
Lewis structure for both water and CO2.

00:18:31.499 --> 00:18:32.849
