00:18:33.070 --> 00:18:37.790
The calorimetry experiment was also a very
data heavy lab, which had a lot of workup.

00:18:38.470 --> 00:18:42.370
The good thing about the calorimetry lab,
I guess, is that it was actually fast.

00:18:42.850 --> 00:18:46.240
So meaning it's something that is
doable within a five minute period,

00:18:46.250 --> 00:18:49.660
because all you were doing was
mixing different solutions together

00:18:49.910 --> 00:18:51.730
and seeing, is there a heat change?

00:18:52.030 --> 00:18:54.330
So there was a couple of different
things that were possible.

00:18:54.670 --> 00:18:58.650
Now with calorimetry, if you don't
really remember it really well, I did

00:18:58.650 --> 00:19:04.080
make a video just on calorimetry and I
did try to title it lab based examples

00:19:04.080 --> 00:19:08.260
because I went through very similar
examples to what you did in the lab

00:19:08.260 --> 00:19:10.810
to try to help you with calculations.

00:19:10.810 --> 00:19:14.574
And So with this thing, the whole
idea is being able to recognize

00:19:14.585 --> 00:19:16.935
first what the system looked like.

00:19:17.235 --> 00:19:21.705
So this one, we had two coffee cups, which
were sitting in nested in each other.

00:19:22.125 --> 00:19:25.755
And then we had a little
cardboard lid on the top here.

00:19:26.164 --> 00:19:29.344
And what you did is you mixed
a bunch of solutions together.

00:19:29.644 --> 00:19:34.445
You would mix them together and you'd be
measuring what the temperature change was.

00:19:34.774 --> 00:19:38.255
Whether the temperature went
up or went down told you

00:19:38.255 --> 00:19:39.735
some stuff about the science.

00:19:40.045 --> 00:19:42.715
So, what are some things that I can
think of that you would need to know?

00:19:43.215 --> 00:19:46.894
So, the first one is, well, make sure
you really know how to use the MCAT

00:19:46.935 --> 00:19:49.314
equation, which I wrote up here, right?

00:19:49.445 --> 00:19:54.465
Knowing M is mass, C was the specific
heat, which usually probably a

00:19:54.465 --> 00:19:55.725
lot of you forgot about that.

00:19:56.044 --> 00:20:00.574
That was how much heat is
required to heat up one gram of a

00:20:00.574 --> 00:20:02.945
substance by one degree Celsius.

00:20:02.945 --> 00:20:06.330
So, and then times that by
the change in temperature.

00:20:06.810 --> 00:20:09.830
So that is something you have to do,
and we'll go through an example of that.

00:20:11.080 --> 00:20:14.000
Now I would recommend knowing
your units for this one.

00:20:14.330 --> 00:20:19.710
Mass is in grams, C is usually in
joules over grams over Celsius,

00:20:20.019 --> 00:20:21.540
and then T is in Celsius.

00:20:23.019 --> 00:20:26.229
Now with, make sure you know how
to read a thermometer, right?

00:20:26.339 --> 00:20:29.250
Once again, tick marks and
sig figs are important.

00:20:29.550 --> 00:20:33.880
They will ding you on wrong answers
when it comes to answering these.

00:20:34.439 --> 00:20:38.939
Now, the other thing is knowing how
to do a change in temperature, right?

00:20:39.330 --> 00:20:43.430
Whenever we have a delta, that's
a displacement or a difference.

00:20:43.689 --> 00:20:45.819
So that's final minus initial, right?

00:20:45.819 --> 00:20:46.810
It's always in that order.

00:20:47.649 --> 00:20:51.709
Now the other thing is to keep in mind
is remember exothermic and endothermic.

00:20:52.080 --> 00:20:56.170
If the temperature goes up, meaning
it's releasing heat, it's exothermic.

00:20:56.600 --> 00:20:59.669
If the temperature is getting
colder, that means it's absorbing

00:20:59.679 --> 00:21:01.759
heat and it's going down, right?

00:21:01.759 --> 00:21:03.320
So that's what you're going to observe.

00:21:03.595 --> 00:21:05.155
when you're looking at those numbers.

00:21:05.625 --> 00:21:08.885
So the big thing is like, know
how one thing that's easier to do

00:21:08.885 --> 00:21:11.965
is like, maybe you might have to
make this and do it observations.

00:21:12.415 --> 00:21:16.265
But another one is, can you manipulate the
data, which is usually the harder part.

00:21:16.655 --> 00:21:20.224
So when it comes to trying to
calculate these, right, you might

00:21:20.224 --> 00:21:23.925
be given a data set and you need to
be, and you're told solve for heat.

00:21:24.175 --> 00:21:25.345
Could you do it, right?

00:21:25.345 --> 00:21:26.635
So you got to be given the values.

00:21:27.004 --> 00:21:31.014
So the first thing is you always take
your total mass of the solution, so that

00:21:31.014 --> 00:21:33.504
will be your mass in that MCAT equation.

00:21:33.504 --> 00:21:36.615
So we have QMC delta T.

00:21:37.144 --> 00:21:40.645
So what we're going to do is
we're going to take our mass,

00:21:40.654 --> 00:21:42.165
which in this case is 40.

00:21:42.975 --> 00:21:44.284
1234 grams.

00:21:44.895 --> 00:21:47.835
We're going to multiply
that by C, which is 4.

00:21:47.895 --> 00:21:55.205
184 joules grams C, and you want to write
down all of your units while you do this,

00:21:55.215 --> 00:21:58.635
so that way you get the maximum number
of points while you're going through it.

00:21:59.205 --> 00:22:03.465
And then you're going to write down
times Celsius, and then you're going

00:22:03.465 --> 00:22:06.295
to notice that, hey, your units
are cancelling, so that's going to

00:22:06.305 --> 00:22:09.924
give you an idea that you're doing
these correctly, so that's good.

00:22:10.555 --> 00:22:13.495
So once you multiply 40.

00:22:13.495 --> 00:22:14.854
1234 times 4.

00:22:14.855 --> 00:22:18.725
184 times 30, you're
going to get about 5036.

00:22:19.635 --> 00:22:21.795
33.

00:22:22.635 --> 00:22:23.085
Joules.

00:22:23.575 --> 00:22:25.815
So once again, is this a correct answer?

00:22:26.195 --> 00:22:27.835
Technically, from your calculator, yes.

00:22:28.365 --> 00:22:30.705
But we need to show the correct sig figs.

00:22:31.055 --> 00:22:33.755
So one thing you'll always look for
is the one with the smallest number.

00:22:34.015 --> 00:22:36.005
So we have three sig figs
from your temperature.

00:22:36.064 --> 00:22:38.375
So we got to round it to three sig figs.

00:22:38.794 --> 00:22:44.095
So what this is going to be is
5040 joules, or you can show it in

00:22:44.095 --> 00:22:48.945
kilojoules or scientific notation,
whichever one, you know, it makes

00:22:48.975 --> 00:22:50.665
essentially like makes you happy here.

00:22:51.084 --> 00:22:52.995
So I'm sure all of them will be accepted.

00:22:53.304 --> 00:22:55.735
But it just kind of depends on
which one you want to aim for.

