and then sodium sulfate.

00:32:59.635 --> 00:33:03.165
is here, and what they're
going to be doing is mixing

00:33:03.165 --> 00:33:05.675
together to make a new compound.

00:33:06.125 --> 00:33:09.645
So the first thing we always do is
we assign our letters to each ion.

00:33:09.645 --> 00:33:13.604
So barium is ion, chlorine is an ion,
we have sodium, and then sulfate.

00:33:13.945 --> 00:33:16.154
So it's AB plus CD.

00:33:16.534 --> 00:33:17.625
So what is that?

00:33:17.665 --> 00:33:20.105
That's going to be a double
replacement reaction.

00:33:20.575 --> 00:33:25.415
So what we're going to have bond together
is the inside is going to bond and then

00:33:25.415 --> 00:33:27.675
the outside is going to bond together.

00:33:28.285 --> 00:33:31.295
Now the thing to remember,
cation is always written first.

00:33:31.505 --> 00:33:32.775
So we'll take sodium.

00:33:33.215 --> 00:33:35.964
We do not write the subscript here.

00:33:35.965 --> 00:33:39.570
We just bring the ion, And then
we same thing with chlorine

00:33:39.570 --> 00:33:41.130
will bring that over as well.

00:33:41.130 --> 00:33:42.280
So just the ion.

00:33:42.620 --> 00:33:45.480
So notice how they're, they're
balanced with their charges.

00:33:45.480 --> 00:33:47.090
So we don't have to worry about swap drop.

00:33:47.750 --> 00:33:50.530
And then we'll bring
over barium and sulfate.

00:33:50.540 --> 00:33:51.660
So B a.

00:33:52.210 --> 00:33:56.250
SO4, and that's 2 plus and
2 minus, so that's balanced

00:33:56.430 --> 00:33:57.660
in terms of charge as well.

00:33:58.360 --> 00:34:02.610
Now the next part is we're going
to do our, balance our formula.

00:34:03.090 --> 00:34:06.410
So you'll notice is that there's
two sodiums and two chlorines on the

00:34:06.410 --> 00:34:11.510
left, so what we're going to do is put
two on the right, and now we have a

00:34:11.610 --> 00:34:16.360
balanced complete molecular equation
once we remove all those charges.

00:34:17.810 --> 00:34:20.410
Now the next part is using
your solubility rules.

00:34:20.775 --> 00:34:24.105
So the first thing is that we have
barium chloride and sodium sulfate.

00:34:24.525 --> 00:34:28.425
So one thing you'll see is if we
look for our different ions, so we

00:34:28.435 --> 00:34:30.885
have chlorine here and then sulfate.

00:34:31.305 --> 00:34:33.935
It says chlorine is soluble
except when it's bound to some

00:34:33.935 --> 00:34:35.585
heavy metals, so that one's good.

00:34:35.980 --> 00:34:39.220
And then we have sulfate is
soluble except when it's bound

00:34:39.220 --> 00:34:41.290
to calcium, strontium, or barium.

00:34:41.680 --> 00:34:44.980
So it's soluble is sodium, but not
with barium, which was our other one.

00:34:45.560 --> 00:34:50.600
So with these, what we can see is,
is what was soluble is the barium.

00:34:50.605 --> 00:34:53.930
Chloride is aqueous,
sodium sulfate was aqueous.

00:34:54.830 --> 00:34:56.510
Uh, sodium chloride is aqueous.

00:34:56.720 --> 00:35:01.850
But our last one, which was the barium
sulfate, is a solid, that one's insoluble.

00:35:02.560 --> 00:35:06.220
So now we're going to break
everything down into its ions.

00:35:06.760 --> 00:35:11.610
So the barium will break down into an
ion, so we put barium 2 and we'll have

00:35:11.620 --> 00:35:14.940
two chlorines, and two so sodiums.

00:35:14.940 --> 00:35:20.280
It's like we're smashing all these pieces
because they are soluble in water, right?

00:35:20.280 --> 00:35:21.780
So everything's breaking down.

00:35:22.550 --> 00:35:27.299
And then the only thing that stays
together are solids and gases.

00:35:27.350 --> 00:35:28.720
They stay together.

00:35:29.300 --> 00:35:34.300
So now when we have that, oops, Uh,
when those things stay together,

00:35:34.900 --> 00:35:38.700
you will pretty much, you're
going to leave them there for the

00:35:38.700 --> 00:35:41.100
very last part of the reaction.

00:35:41.650 --> 00:35:44.569
So we're canceling out anything
that's a spectator, that's

00:35:44.570 --> 00:35:46.669
identical on the left and the right.

00:35:47.060 --> 00:35:49.749
So the things that you'll see is
that, okay, there's two chlorines,

00:35:50.059 --> 00:35:52.780
there's two sodiums, they're
the same, they're just watching.

00:35:53.340 --> 00:35:58.360
So everything else stays
in the net ionic equation.

00:35:58.720 --> 00:36:00.810
So there's barium, sulfate.

00:36:01.310 --> 00:36:05.740
And then there's, that's all leading
to barium sulfate, and that's how you

00:36:05.740 --> 00:36:08.880
would write a net ionic equation, right?

